  ($tfq$q-tf004-38$tfq$, $tfq$terraform-associate-004$tfq$, $tfq$Hard$tfq$, $tfq$single$tfq$, $tfq$A variable has a default, a value in terraform.tfvars, and a value supplied with -var on the command line. Which value is used for that CLI-driven run?$tfq$, $tfq$The declared default because configuration has highest precedence$tfq$, $tfq$The terraform.tfvars value because it is loaded automatically$tfq$, $tfq$The command-line -var value because it has higher precedence$tfq$, $tfq$Terraform stops because the variable has more than one value source$tfq$, NULL, NULL, 2, ARRAY[2]::INTEGER[], $tfq$For the Terraform CLI variable-loading order, command-line -var and -var-file assignments take precedence over automatically loaded tfvars values and declared defaults.$tfq$, $tfq$tf004-configuration$tfq$),
  ($tfq$q-tf004-39$tfq$, $tfq$terraform-associate-004$tfq$, $tfq$Medium$tfq$, $tfq$single$tfq$, $tfq$A module needs a named collection whose values each contain a CIDR string and a Boolean public flag. Which type constraint best represents this input?$tfq$, $tfq$list(string)$tfq$, $tfq$map(object({ cidr = string, public = bool }))$tfq$, $tfq$tuple([string, bool])$tfq$, $tfq$set(map(string))$tfq$, NULL, NULL, 1, ARRAY[1]::INTEGER[], $tfq$A map provides stable names as keys, while each object enforces the cidr string and public Boolean attributes. The other types do not model a named collection of consistently shaped records.$tfq$, $tfq$tf004-configuration$tfq$),
  ($tfq$q-tf004-40$tfq$, $tfq$terraform-associate-004$tfq$, $tfq$Hard$tfq$, $tfq$single$tfq$, $tfq$Which expression returns only enabled service names from var.services, where each map value has an enabled Boolean attribute?$tfq$, $tfq$[for name, service in var.services : name if service.enabled]$tfq$, $tfq$[for name in var.services : service.enabled => name]$tfq$, $tfq$for_each(var.services, service.enabled, name)$tfq$, $tfq$filter(var.services[*].name, enabled = true)$tfq$, NULL, NULL, 0, ARRAY[0]::INTEGER[], $tfq$A for expression can transform each map element to its name and use an if clause to filter out values whose enabled attribute is false.$tfq$, $tfq$tf004-configuration$tfq$),
  ($tfq$q-tf004-41$tfq$, $tfq$terraform-associate-004$tfq$, $tfq$Hard$tfq$, $tfq$single$tfq$, $tfq$A resource must be created only when an input assumption is valid. Which custom condition is the closest fit?$tfq$, $tfq$A postcondition that is evaluated only after every future run$tfq$, $tfq$A precondition attached to the resource lifecycle$tfq$, $tfq$A check block that automatically changes the invalid input$tfq$, $tfq$A variable output that replaces the resource dependency$tfq$, NULL, NULL, 1, ARRAY[1]::INTEGER[], $tfq$A resource precondition checks an assumption before Terraform performs the associated resource operation and can stop the operation with a useful error message.$tfq$, $tfq$tf004-configuration$tfq$),
  ($tfq$q-tf004-42$tfq$, $tfq$terraform-associate-004$tfq$, $tfq$Hard$tfq$, $tfq$multiple$tfq$, $tfq$Which two statements about supported write-only resource arguments are correct? Select two.$tfq$, $tfq$Their configured values are not persisted in plan or state files$tfq$, $tfq$Every resource argument becomes write-only when marked sensitive$tfq$, $tfq$Provider support determines whether an argument is write-only$tfq$, $tfq$They can be read later with terraform state show$tfq$, $tfq$They remove the need to secure provider and Terraform logs$tfq$, NULL, 0, ARRAY[0, 2]::INTEGER[], $tfq$A provider schema defines supported write-only arguments, and Terraform does not persist their values in plan or state artifacts. They are different from sensitive values and do not make logging automatically safe.$tfq$, $tfq$tf004-configuration$tfq$),
  ($tfq$q-tf004-43$tfq$, $tfq$terraform-associate-004$tfq$, $tfq$Medium$tfq$, $tfq$single$tfq$, $tfq$A root module needs the subnet ID created inside a child module. What should form the child module interface for this value?$tfq$, $tfq$A child output referenced as module.network.subnet_id$tfq$, $tfq$A direct reference to the child resource local address$tfq$, $tfq$A backend block copied from the child into the caller$tfq$, $tfq$A provider lock entry exported by the child module$tfq$, NULL, NULL, 0, ARRAY[0]::INTEGER[], $tfq$The child module declares an output and the caller reads it through the module object. A caller cannot directly address a resource inside the child module as if it were in the same scope.$tfq$, $tfq$tf004-modules$tfq$),
  ($tfq$q-tf004-44$tfq$, $tfq$terraform-associate-004$tfq$, $tfq$Hard$tfq$, $tfq$single$tfq$, $tfq$A module block uses a local path source and also sets version = "~> 2.0". What is the problem?$tfq$, $tfq$Local modules cannot declare input variables or output values$tfq$, $tfq$The version argument applies to Registry module sources, not local paths$tfq$, $tfq$Local modules must be downloaded with terraform providers mirror$tfq$, $tfq$The version constraint must be moved into the provider configuration$tfq$, NULL, NULL, 1, ARRAY[1]::INTEGER[], $tfq$The module version argument is supported for Registry module sources. A local-path module uses the files at that path and is not selected through a Registry version constraint.$tfq$, $tfq$tf004-modules$tfq$),
  ($tfq$q-tf004-45$tfq$, $tfq$terraform-associate-004$tfq$, $tfq$Medium$tfq$, $tfq$multiple$tfq$, $tfq$Which two characteristics describe the default local backend? Select two.$tfq$, $tfq$It stores state on the local filesystem by default$tfq$, $tfq$It automatically provides shared remote collaboration$tfq$, $tfq$It performs Terraform operations on the local machine$tfq$, $tfq$It automatically encrypts state with HCP Terraform keys$tfq$, $tfq$It requires a cloud object-storage bucket for every workspace$tfq$, NULL, 0, ARRAY[0, 2]::INTEGER[], $tfq$The local backend stores state locally and executes operations locally. It does not by itself provide remote shared state, hosted encryption, or an object-storage requirement.$tfq$, $tfq$tf004-state$tfq$),
  ($tfq$q-tf004-46$tfq$, $tfq$terraform-associate-004$tfq$, $tfq$Hard$tfq$, $tfq$single$tfq$, $tfq$Why can a backend block not use normal input-variable references for all of its settings?$tfq$, $tfq$Backend initialization occurs before normal expression evaluation is available$tfq$, $tfq$Backends can only store state for configurations without any variables$tfq$, $tfq$Backend settings are copied from the provider dependency lock file$tfq$, $tfq$Terraform evaluates backend settings only after resources are applied$tfq$, NULL, NULL, 0, ARRAY[0]::INTEGER[], $tfq$Terraform must initialize the backend before it can perform the normal evaluation and planning workflow. Backend configuration therefore has restrictions and can also receive partial settings during initialization.$tfq$, $tfq$tf004-state$tfq$),
  ($tfq$q-tf004-47$tfq$, $tfq$terraform-associate-004$tfq$, $tfq$Hard$tfq$, $tfq$single$tfq$, $tfq$A team wants Terraform to stop managing an object without destroying the remote object, and wants the decision recorded in configuration. Which construct should they consider?$tfq$, $tfq$A removed block$tfq$, $tfq$A dynamic block$tfq$, $tfq$A check block$tfq$, $tfq$A terraform block$tfq$, NULL, NULL, 0, ARRAY[0]::INTEGER[], $tfq$A removed block can record that a resource is intentionally removed from Terraform management and, when configured not to destroy, preserve the remote object while removing it from state.$tfq$, $tfq$tf004-state$tfq$),
  ($tfq$q-tf004-48$tfq$, $tfq$terraform-associate-004$tfq$, $tfq$Hard$tfq$, $tfq$single$tfq$, $tfq$What advantage does an import block provide compared with relying only on a one-time terraform import CLI command?$tfq$, $tfq$It records the import request in reviewable configuration$tfq$, $tfq$It guarantees that generated configuration needs no review$tfq$, $tfq$It moves the imported object into the provider account$tfq$, $tfq$It prevents Terraform from refreshing the imported object$tfq$, NULL, NULL, 0, ARRAY[0]::INTEGER[], $tfq$An import block makes the intended import part of configuration, so it can be planned and reviewed with the rest of the change. Any generated or authored resource configuration still requires careful review.$tfq$, $tfq$tf004-maintenance$tfq$),
  ($tfq$q-tf004-49$tfq$, $tfq$terraform-associate-004$tfq$, $tfq$Medium$tfq$, $tfq$multiple$tfq$, $tfq$Which two statements correctly describe HCP Terraform projects and variable sets? Select two.$tfq$, $tfq$Projects can group related workspaces for organization and access control$tfq$, $tfq$Variable sets can provide reusable variables to selected workspaces or projects$tfq$, $tfq$Projects replace provider plugins with built-in cloud API clients$tfq$, $tfq$Variable sets are committed automatically to every linked repository$tfq$, $tfq$Projects force all workspaces to share one Terraform state file$tfq$, NULL, 0, ARRAY[0, 1]::INTEGER[], $tfq$Projects organize related workspaces and can support project-level permissions. Variable sets let teams reuse common variable definitions across selected scopes without forcing workspaces to share state.$tfq$, $tfq$tf004-hcp$tfq$),
  ($tfq$q-tf004-50$tfq$, $tfq$terraform-associate-004$tfq$, $tfq$Hard$tfq$, $tfq$multiple$tfq$, $tfq$Which two HCP Terraform features reduce manual coordination between connected workspaces and cloud credentials? Select two.$tfq$, $tfq$Run triggers that queue downstream workspace runs$tfq$, $tfq$Dynamic provider credentials issued for a run$tfq$, $tfq$A shared plaintext credentials file committed to VCS$tfq$, $tfq$One state file reused by every project workspace$tfq$, $tfq$Automatic approval of every policy failure$tfq$, NULL, 0, ARRAY[0, 1]::INTEGER[], $tfq$Run triggers coordinate dependent workspace runs, while dynamic provider credentials can issue short-lived credentials for an HCP Terraform run. The other options weaken isolation or governance.$tfq$, $tfq$tf004-hcp$tfq$),
  ($tfq$q-tf004-51$tfq$, $tfq$terraform-associate-004$tfq$, $tfq$Easy$tfq$, $tfq$single$tfq$, $tfq$Which statement best describes infrastructure as code in a Terraform workflow?$tfq$, $tfq$Infrastructure is defined in machine-readable configuration and managed through repeatable operations$tfq$, $tfq$Infrastructure is created manually and Terraform records screenshots of the completed environment$tfq$, $tfq$Infrastructure is stored entirely inside provider plugins rather than in configuration files$tfq$, $tfq$Infrastructure is changed through tickets while Terraform monitors the approval queue$tfq$, NULL, NULL, 0, ARRAY[0]::INTEGER[], $tfq$Infrastructure as code represents desired infrastructure in machine-readable files that can be reviewed, versioned, and used in repeatable workflows. It does not mean recording or automating a manual ticket process.$tfq$, $tfq$tf004-iac$tfq$),
  ($tfq$q-tf004-52$tfq$, $tfq$terraform-associate-004$tfq$, $tfq$Medium$tfq$, $tfq$multiple$tfq$, $tfq$Which two outcomes are direct advantages of an infrastructure-as-code workflow? Select two.$tfq$, $tfq$Changes can be reviewed through version-control history$tfq$, $tfq$Identical configuration always produces identical provider-generated IDs$tfq$, $tfq$Environments can be created through a repeatable process$tfq$, $tfq$Remote users are prevented from making manual changes$tfq$, $tfq$Provider API outages no longer affect infrastructure changes$tfq$, NULL, 0, ARRAY[0, 2]::INTEGER[], $tfq$Version control makes configuration changes auditable and reviewable, while reusable configuration supports repeatable environment creation. IaC cannot guarantee generated IDs, eliminate API outages, or technically prevent all manual changes.$tfq$, $tfq$tf004-iac$tfq$),
  ($tfq$q-tf004-53$tfq$, $tfq$terraform-associate-004$tfq$, $tfq$Medium$tfq$, $tfq$single$tfq$, $tfq$A company manages on-premises virtualization, a public cloud, and a SaaS monitoring platform. What allows one Terraform workflow to cover this hybrid environment?$tfq$, $tfq$A single state file converts every platform into the same service API$tfq$, $tfq$Separate providers expose each platform through Terraform resources and data sources$tfq$, $tfq$The local backend copies all on-premises objects into the public cloud$tfq$, $tfq$Terraform modules remove the need for platform-specific authentication$tfq$, NULL, NULL, 1, ARRAY[1]::INTEGER[], $tfq$Terraform uses platform-specific providers behind a consistent configuration and workflow model. Each provider still communicates with its own API and requires appropriate authentication.$tfq$, $tfq$tf004-iac$tfq$),
  ($tfq$q-tf004-54$tfq$, $tfq$terraform-associate-004$tfq$, $tfq$Hard$tfq$, $tfq$single$tfq$, $tfq$A root module permits AWS provider versions >= 5.0 and < 6.0, while a child module requires >= 5.30. Which versions can Terraform select?$tfq$, $tfq$Any version from 5.0 upward because root constraints override child constraints$tfq$, $tfq$A version from 5.30 up to, but not including, 6.0$tfq$, $tfq$Exactly version 5.30 because the child module is more specific$tfq$, $tfq$No version because different modules cannot constrain one provider$tfq$, NULL, NULL, 1, ARRAY[1]::INTEGER[], $tfq$Terraform selects one provider version compatible with every module requirement. The intersection is version 5.30 or newer but still below 6.0; the lock file can then record the selected version.$tfq$, $tfq$tf004-fundamentals$tfq$),
  ($tfq$q-tf004-55$tfq$, $tfq$terraform-associate-004$tfq$, $tfq$Medium$tfq$, $tfq$single$tfq$, $tfq$What information does a Terraform provider schema supply to Terraform Core?$tfq$, $tfq$Supported resource and data-source types with their arguments and attributes$tfq$, $tfq$The complete desired configuration for every resource in the workspace$tfq$, $tfq$The team permissions used to approve runs in HCP Terraform$tfq$, $tfq$The version-control history used to calculate infrastructure drift$tfq$, NULL, NULL, 0, ARRAY[0]::INTEGER[], $tfq$A provider plugin exposes schemas and implements operations for its resource and data-source types. Terraform Core uses that information to validate configuration and coordinate planning and applying.$tfq$, $tfq$tf004-fundamentals$tfq$),
  ($tfq$q-tf004-56$tfq$, $tfq$terraform-associate-004$tfq$, $tfq$Hard$tfq$, $tfq$multiple$tfq$, $tfq$A root module configures default aws and aliased aws.dr providers. Which two statements are correct? Select two.$tfq$, $tfq$A resource can select aws.dr with its provider meta-argument$tfq$, $tfq$Every child module automatically inherits all aliased configurations$tfq$, $tfq$A module call can explicitly map a child provider name to aws.dr$tfq$, $tfq$The alias changes the provider source address to a new provider$tfq$, $tfq$Each alias requires a separate provider version in the lock file$tfq$, NULL, 0, ARRAY[0, 2]::INTEGER[], $tfq$Resources can select an alias with provider = aws.dr, and callers can pass configurations through a module providers map. Aliases are configurations of the same provider and are not all inherited automatically.$tfq$, $tfq$tf004-fundamentals$tfq$),
  ($tfq$q-tf004-57$tfq$, $tfq$terraform-associate-004$tfq$, $tfq$Medium$tfq$, $tfq$single$tfq$, $tfq$Why does Terraform normally refresh information about managed objects before proposing changes?$tfq$, $tfq$To update the dependency lock file with newer provider checksums$tfq$, $tfq$To compare recorded state with current remote object information$tfq$, $tfq$To rewrite all variable values from the provider configuration$tfq$, $tfq$To import every remote object that matches a resource type$tfq$, NULL, NULL, 1, ARRAY[1]::INTEGER[], $tfq$Refreshing lets Terraform compare prior state and current remote values before calculating the plan. It does not discover and import unrelated objects or update provider dependencies.$tfq$, $tfq$tf004-fundamentals$tfq$),
  ($tfq$q-tf004-58$tfq$, $tfq$terraform-associate-004$tfq$, $tfq$Easy$tfq$, $tfq$single$tfq$, $tfq$Which sequence most closely follows the core Terraform workflow for a new configuration?$tfq$, $tfq$Write, initialize, plan, review, and apply$tfq$, $tfq$Apply, initialize, write, review, and destroy$tfq$, $tfq$Initialize, destroy, import, format, and apply$tfq$, $tfq$Write, apply, initialize, refresh, and review$tfq$, NULL, NULL, 0, ARRAY[0]::INTEGER[], $tfq$The core workflow is to write configuration, initialize the working directory, generate and review a plan, and then apply the approved changes. Validation and formatting support this workflow.$tfq$, $tfq$tf004-workflow$tfq$),
  ($tfq$q-tf004-59$tfq$, $tfq$terraform-associate-004$tfq$, $tfq$Medium$tfq$, $tfq$single$tfq$, $tfq$A backend configuration changed, and the team wants to accept the new configuration without copying state from the previously configured backend. Which initialization option fits?$tfq$, $tfq$terraform init -upgrade$tfq$, $tfq$terraform init -reconfigure$tfq$, $tfq$terraform init -migrate-state$tfq$, $tfq$terraform init -get=false$tfq$, NULL, NULL, 1, ARRAY[1]::INTEGER[], $tfq$terraform init -reconfigure disregards the previously saved backend configuration and initializes using the new settings without performing state migration. Migration is a distinct, deliberate operation.$tfq$, $tfq$tf004-workflow$tfq$),
  ($tfq$q-tf004-60$tfq$, $tfq$terraform-associate-004$tfq$, $tfq$Medium$tfq$, $tfq$multiple$tfq$, $tfq$Which two statements about terraform validate are correct? Select two.$tfq$, $tfq$It checks configuration syntax and internal consistency$tfq$, $tfq$It verifies that cloud credentials can create every resource$tfq$, $tfq$It can be run without contacting configured remote services$tfq$, $tfq$It changes remote objects to match the configuration$tfq$, $tfq$It replaces the need to review an execution plan$tfq$, NULL, 0, ARRAY[0, 2]::INTEGER[], $tfq$terraform validate checks syntax and internal consistency and does not validate remote APIs or credentials. An initialized directory may be needed for providers and modules, but validation itself does not plan or apply infrastructure.$tfq$, $tfq$tf004-workflow$tfq$),
  ($tfq$q-tf004-61$tfq$, $tfq$terraform-associate-004$tfq$, $tfq$Hard$tfq$, $tfq$single$tfq$, $tfq$In automation, terraform plan -detailed-exitcode returns exit code 2. What does that result mean?$tfq$, $tfq$The plan failed because configuration could not be parsed$tfq$, $tfq$The plan succeeded and proposed at least one change$tfq$, $tfq$The plan succeeded and found no infrastructure changes$tfq$, $tfq$The plan was applied and state was updated successfully$tfq$, NULL, NULL, 1, ARRAY[1]::INTEGER[], $tfq$With -detailed-exitcode, 0 means success with an empty diff, 1 means an error, and 2 means success with proposed changes. The command still only creates a plan.$tfq$, $tfq$tf004-workflow$tfq$),
  ($tfq$q-tf004-62$tfq$, $tfq$terraform-associate-004$tfq$, $tfq$Medium$tfq$, $tfq$single$tfq$, $tfq$What happens when terraform apply is run without supplying a previously saved plan?$tfq$, $tfq$Terraform creates a new plan and normally asks for approval before applying it$tfq$, $tfq$Terraform applies the most recent plan found anywhere in the working directory$tfq$, $tfq$Terraform skips planning and sends configuration directly to each provider$tfq$, $tfq$Terraform applies only output changes and leaves resources unchanged$tfq$, NULL, NULL, 0, ARRAY[0]::INTEGER[], $tfq$Without a saved plan file, terraform apply creates a fresh execution plan and normally asks the user to approve it. A saved plan is required when the exact earlier plan must be applied.$tfq$, $tfq$tf004-workflow$tfq$),
  ($tfq$q-tf004-63$tfq$, $tfq$terraform-associate-004$tfq$, $tfq$Medium$tfq$, $tfq$single$tfq$, $tfq$How does terraform destroy determine which managed objects to propose for deletion?$tfq$, $tfq$It proposes destroy actions for objects represented by the current configuration and state$tfq$, $tfq$It lists every object accessible through the configured provider credentials$tfq$, $tfq$It deletes provider plugins and assumes remote objects are deleted separately$tfq$, $tfq$It removes only objects created during the immediately preceding apply$tfq$, NULL, NULL, 0, ARRAY[0]::INTEGER[], $tfq$terraform destroy is a convenience form of planning and applying a destroy operation for managed objects in the current configuration and state. It does not enumerate every accessible account resource.$tfq$, $tfq$tf004-workflow$tfq$),
  ($tfq$q-tf004-64$tfq$, $tfq$terraform-associate-004$tfq$, $tfq$Easy$tfq$, $tfq$single$tfq$, $tfq$What is the effect of running terraform fmt -recursive in a configuration directory?$tfq$, $tfq$It formats Terraform files in the directory and its subdirectories$tfq$, $tfq$It validates remote provider credentials in every child module$tfq$, $tfq$It generates and saves one plan for each configuration file$tfq$, $tfq$It upgrades modules and providers to their newest versions$tfq$, NULL, NULL, 0, ARRAY[0]::INTEGER[], $tfq$terraform fmt rewrites Terraform configuration into canonical style, and -recursive includes subdirectories. Formatting does not validate credentials, produce plans, or upgrade dependencies.$tfq$, $tfq$tf004-workflow$tfq$),
  ($tfq$q-tf004-65$tfq$, $tfq$terraform-associate-004$tfq$, $tfq$Medium$tfq$, $tfq$single$tfq$, $tfq$A configuration reads the current AWS account identity and creates a storage bucket. Which pairing uses the appropriate block types?$tfq$, $tfq$Use a data block for the account identity and a resource block for the bucket$tfq$, $tfq$Use a resource block for the account identity and a data block for the bucket$tfq$, $tfq$Use output blocks for both because neither value belongs in configuration$tfq$, $tfq$Use import blocks for both so Terraform can discover their schemas$tfq$, NULL, NULL, 0, ARRAY[0]::INTEGER[], $tfq$A data source reads existing information such as the current caller identity, while a resource block declares an object whose lifecycle Terraform should manage.$tfq$, $tfq$tf004-configuration$tfq$),
  ($tfq$q-tf004-66$tfq$, $tfq$terraform-associate-004$tfq$, $tfq$Medium$tfq$, $tfq$single$tfq$, $tfq$A subnet argument references aws_vpc.main.id. What additional behavior does Terraform derive from that reference?$tfq$, $tfq$An implicit dependency that places the VPC before the subnet$tfq$, $tfq$A provider alias that moves the subnet into another region$tfq$, $tfq$A lifecycle rule that prevents the VPC from being replaced$tfq$, $tfq$A state lock that applies only to those two resources$tfq$, NULL, NULL, 0, ARRAY[0]::INTEGER[], $tfq$The attribute reference both supplies the VPC ID and creates an implicit graph dependency. Terraform can therefore order the VPC operation before the dependent subnet operation.$tfq$, $tfq$tf004-configuration$tfq$),
  ($tfq$q-tf004-67$tfq$, $tfq$terraform-associate-004$tfq$, $tfq$Medium$tfq$, $tfq$multiple$tfq$, $tfq$Which two statements about root-module output values are correct? Select two.$tfq$, $tfq$They can expose selected values after an apply$tfq$, $tfq$They automatically become input variables in every child module$tfq$, $tfq$Sensitive outputs are redacted in normal CLI output$tfq$, $tfq$They are never recorded in Terraform state$tfq$, $tfq$They can only refer to literal string values$tfq$, NULL, 0, ARRAY[0, 2]::INTEGER[], $tfq$Root outputs expose selected values and can be marked sensitive to redact normal display. Output values are stored in state, can use expressions of many types, and do not automatically become child inputs.$tfq$, $tfq$tf004-configuration$tfq$),
  ($tfq$q-tf004-68$tfq$, $tfq$terraform-associate-004$tfq$, $tfq$Medium$tfq$, $tfq$single$tfq$, $tfq$A variable must contain exactly three values in a fixed order: a string name, a number port, and a Boolean enabled flag. Which type best describes it?$tfq$, $tfq$tuple([string, number, bool])$tfq$, $tfq$list(string)$tfq$, $tfq$map(any)$tfq$, $tfq$set(object({ value = string }))$tfq$, NULL, NULL, 0, ARRAY[0]::INTEGER[], $tfq$A tuple can require a fixed number of elements with a distinct type at each position. Lists and sets contain elements of a common type, while the proposed map has no fixed positional structure.$tfq$, $tfq$tf004-configuration$tfq$),
