  ($tfq$q-tf004-74$tfq$, $tfq$terraform-associate-004$tfq$, $tfq$Hard$tfq$, $tfq$single$tfq$, $tfq$What is the primary purpose of a dynamic block inside a resource?$tfq$, $tfq$Generate repeated nested blocks from a collection or expression$tfq$, $tfq$Choose provider versions dynamically during terraform apply$tfq$, $tfq$Create new resource types that the provider does not implement$tfq$, $tfq$Read arbitrary remote objects without declaring a data source$tfq$, NULL, NULL, 0, ARRAY[0]::INTEGER[], $tfq$A dynamic block generates repeated nested configuration blocks using collection data. It cannot generate top-level resource blocks or create provider capabilities that do not exist.$tfq$, $tfq$tf004-configuration$tfq$),
  ($tfq$q-tf004-75$tfq$, $tfq$terraform-associate-004$tfq$, $tfq$Hard$tfq$, $tfq$single$tfq$, $tfq$A module input uses object({ name = string, description = optional(string, "managed") }). What happens when a caller omits description?$tfq$, $tfq$Terraform supplies the default string "managed" for that attribute$tfq$, $tfq$Terraform rejects the object because every object attribute is required$tfq$, $tfq$Terraform removes the complete object from the module call$tfq$, $tfq$Terraform converts the description attribute into an empty list$tfq$, NULL, NULL, 0, ARRAY[0]::INTEGER[], $tfq$The optional type modifier makes the attribute optional and the second argument supplies its default. The remaining name attribute is still required and must be a string.$tfq$, $tfq$tf004-configuration$tfq$),
  ($tfq$q-tf004-76$tfq$, $tfq$terraform-associate-004$tfq$, $tfq$Hard$tfq$, $tfq$single$tfq$, $tfq$A Vault data source returns a secret and a normal resource argument consumes it. What should the team assume unless supported ephemeral and write-only features prevent persistence?$tfq$, $tfq$The secret may be recorded in Terraform state and state access must be protected$tfq$, $tfq$Vault guarantees the value cannot appear in state under any circumstances$tfq$, $tfq$Terraform replaces the secret with its Vault path before writing state$tfq$, $tfq$The provider lock file encrypts the value with the Vault provider checksum$tfq$, NULL, NULL, 0, ARRAY[0]::INTEGER[], $tfq$Reading a secret from Vault does not automatically keep it out of state when a managed resource stores that value. Teams must understand provider behavior and protect state or use supported non-persistent mechanisms.$tfq$, $tfq$tf004-configuration$tfq$),
  ($tfq$q-tf004-77$tfq$, $tfq$terraform-associate-004$tfq$, $tfq$Medium$tfq$, $tfq$single$tfq$, $tfq$A module source is git::https://example.com/network.git?ref=v2.1.0. What does the ref parameter select?$tfq$, $tfq$The Git revision Terraform should retrieve for the module source$tfq$, $tfq$The provider configuration alias used by module resources$tfq$, $tfq$The Terraform CLI workspace that will hold module state$tfq$, $tfq$The output value returned after the module has been applied$tfq$, NULL, NULL, 0, ARRAY[0]::INTEGER[], $tfq$For a Git module source, ref selects a branch, tag, or commit understood by Git. This is separate from the Registry module version argument and provider configuration.$tfq$, $tfq$tf004-modules$tfq$),
  ($tfq$q-tf004-78$tfq$, $tfq$terraform-associate-004$tfq$, $tfq$Medium$tfq$, $tfq$single$tfq$, $tfq$Can a root module directly reference aws_subnet.private.id when that resource is declared inside a child module named network?$tfq$, $tfq$No; the child must expose the value through an output referenced from module.network$tfq$, $tfq$Yes; every resource address is automatically global across all module scopes$tfq$, $tfq$Yes; but only after copying the child state file into the root directory$tfq$, $tfq$No; child-module resource attributes can never be used outside that module$tfq$, NULL, NULL, 0, ARRAY[0]::INTEGER[], $tfq$Resource names are scoped to their module. The child exposes selected values through outputs, which the caller reads through expressions such as module.network.subnet_id.$tfq$, $tfq$tf004-modules$tfq$),
